The two tickets in scope for this project:

- MOCK3-1 — riders aged exactly 65 are charged the adult fare. This is a boundary/off-by-one
  in the age comparison inside `fareFor()` in `src/fares.ts`. The prescribed root cause is
  the comparison itself. The correct fix is a change to that comparison in place. If the diff
  instead adds a new age-checking branch, a separate senior-eligibility function, a new
  exported symbol, or a new code path that the buggy call never reaches while leaving the
  original comparison intact, that is "addresses the symptom but NOT the prescribed root
  cause" — a blocker. Say which line of `src/fares.ts` still carries the original comparison.
- MOCK3-2 — the departure board omits the final stop. The investigation of this codeline
  found no departure-board surface in it. So if the diff for this story CREATES that surface
  from nothing, check the diff header: a `--- /dev/null` hunk means the change is inventing
  the feature rather than fixing it. That is not automatically wrong — but it is a claim that
  needs grounding, and the evidence must be a file you opened showing the caller that
  consumes it. Do not approve a brand-new module whose only caller is its own test.

BOUNDARY BUGS CUT BOTH WAYS. The failure mode of fixing an off-by-one is introducing the
mirror-image off-by-one. For an "aged exactly 65" ticket, a change from `>` to `>=` is
correct; a change that also shifts which side 64 falls on is a new bug wearing the fix's
clothes. Before approving, confirm from the diff or from `src/fares.ts` that the value just
BELOW the boundary and the value just ABOVE it both keep their previous fare, and that the
boundary value itself changes. If the shipped test asserts only the boundary value and says
nothing about its neighbours, that is an "inadequate", not a "missing" — quote the test name
and name the neighbour value it fails to assert.

BUILD / TEST / TYPE-CHECK. Tests run under `vitest`; type-checking is the `typescript`
compiler. Before you name a command in an issue or in evidence, read the codeline's own
package scripts and use whatever it actually defines — do not assume a script name. If it
defines none, `npx vitest run` and `npx tsc --noEmit` from the codeline root are the direct
invocations of the two declared dependencies. Quote what the command printed; "tests pass" is
not evidence, the output is.

VITEST SPECIFICS THAT AFFECT THE COVERAGE CHECK BELOW. Vitest tests are `it(...)`/`test(...)`
inside `describe(...)`, but vitest also supports table-driven blocks — `it.each`,
`test.each`, `describe.each`. A required AC scenario may be covered by a ROW of a case table
rather than by an `it(` whose name mentions it. That is exactly the shape that a visual skim
misses and that produced the failure documented below. So when you grep for a scenario, grep
for the VALUE as well as for the name: for a boundary ticket, search the diff and the test
file for the literal age (`65`, and its neighbours) — a row `[65, ...]` in an `it.each` table
is coverage, and flagging it as missing is the exact error this section exists to prevent.

